{
  "dom_id": "step-e7902",
  "name": "Example: Solving Inequalities Requiring Division by a Positive Factor",
  "problem": "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n6x^3 + 3x \\lt 0.\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{3}+3x & <0. \\\\ 3x(2{x}^{2}+1) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2x^2 + 1$ is always positive, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x(2{x}^{2}+1)}{2{x}^{2}+1} & <\\frac{0}{2{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ 3x & <0 \\\\ x & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
